## Solution

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains only the limit expression — no solutions, sketches, or answer values. Proceeding.

---

We evaluate
$$L = \lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{\binom{n}{k}}{(k+2)\,n^k}.$$

### Step 1. Pointwise limit of each term

For fixed $k$,
$$\frac{\binom{n}{k}}{n^k}=\frac{n(n-1)\cdots(n-k+1)}{k!\,n^k}=\frac{1}{k!}\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}\!\left(1-\frac{j}{n}\right)\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\frac{1}{k!}.$$

Hence for every fixed $k$,
$$\frac{\binom{n}{k}}{(k+2)\,n^k}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\frac{1}{k!\,(k+2)}.$$

### Step 2. A useful integral representation

Using $\dfrac{1}{k+2}=\displaystyle\int_0^1 x^{k+1}\,dx$,
$$\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k!\,(k+2)}=\int_0^1 x\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^k}{k!}\,dx=\int_0^1 x\,e^x\,dx.$$
Integration by parts gives
$$\int_0^1 x\,e^x\,dx=\Big[x\,e^x-e^x\Big]_0^1=(e-e)-(0-1)=1.$$

### Step 3. Justifying the interchange (dominated convergence for series)

**Upper bound.** Since $n-j\le n$ for every $j\ge 0$,
$$\binom{n}{k}=\frac{n(n-1)\cdots(n-k+1)}{k!}\le\frac{n^k}{k!},\qquad\text{so}\qquad \frac{\binom{n}{k}}{(k+2)\,n^k}\le\frac{1}{k!\,(k+2)}.$$
The dominating series $\sum_{k\ge 0}\frac{1}{k!\,(k+2)}$ converges (to $1$), so
$$S_n:=\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{\binom{n}{k}}{(k+2)\,n^k}\le\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k!\,(k+2)}=1,\qquad\text{hence }\limsup_{n\to\infty}S_n\le 1.$$

**Lower bound.** For any fixed $N$, split $S_n=\sum_{k=0}^{N}+\sum_{k=N+1}^{n}$. The finite sum converges termwise:
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=0}^{N}\frac{\binom{n}{k}}{(k+2)\,n^k}=\sum_{k=0}^{N}\frac{1}{k!\,(k+2)}.$$
Therefore
$$\liminf_{n\to\infty}S_n\ge\sum_{k=0}^{N}\frac{1}{k!\,(k+2)}.$$
Letting $N\to\infty$,
$$\liminf_{n\to\infty}S_n\ge\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k!\,(k+2)}=1.$$

### Step 4. Conclusion

Combining the two bounds,
$$1\le\liminf_{n\to\infty}S_n\le\limsup_{n\to\infty}S_n\le 1,$$
so the limit exists and equals $1$.

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{\binom{n}{k}}{(k+2)\,n^k}=1.}$$

